IMPORT `cat-domain`

-- Cat Act 2011 (WA) s 79 -- the local-law head of power, in two versions.
--
-- WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. The Cat Amendment (Local Laws) Bill 2026 (Bill 53) has
-- exactly one operative clause, and it amends s 79. s 79 sits in Part 6, which
-- the rest of this subject does not encode. So the Bill cannot be read against
-- the encoded Part 2 duties directly: it is read against the power to make local
-- laws, and this module is the arm that makes that possible.
--
-- WHAT IS MODELLED. Not a cat's compliance -- a LOCAL LAW's validity. The
-- question s 79 answers is whether a proposed local law is within power, and
-- that is the question the Bill changes the answer to. The two arms are:
--
--   `section 79(3)(f) as in force authorises the local law`
--   `section 79(3)(f) and (4) as amended by Bill 53 authorise the local law`
--
-- and the predicate that matters is `Bill 53 changes the answer for the local
-- law`, which is true exactly where the amendment does work.
--
-- Sources:
--   the Act    sources/cat-act-2011-00-l0-01.txt (compilation 00-l0-01)
--   the Bill   sources/bill-53-1-cat-amendment-local-laws-2026.txt
--   the EM     sources/em-cat-amendment-local-laws-bill-2026.txt
-- Digests for all three are in registers/source-bundle.json.
--
-- THE BILL IS NOT LAW. As at 1 October 2026 Bill 53 is before the Legislative
-- Council, second reading 16 September 2026. Every rule in the `as amended` arm
-- is a statement about a Bill, not about the law in force, and the names say so.
--
-- NO IMPORTS beyond cat-domain: see the note at the head of cat-domain.l4.

§ `Part 6 -- Local laws`

§§ `79 -- Local laws`

-- s 79(1): "A local government may make local laws prescribing all matters that
-- are required or permitted to be prescribed by a local law, or are necessary or
-- convenient to be so prescribed, for it to perform any of its functions under
-- this Act."
--
-- s 79(3): "WITHOUT LIMITING SUBSECTION (1), a local law may be made as to one
-- or more of the following -- ... (f) specifying places where cats are
-- prohibited absolutely; ...".
--
-- Those four words matter to everything below. s 79(3) is inclusive, so a local
-- law that fits no paragraph of (3) may still be supported by (1) alone. Whether
-- a given law is "necessary or convenient ... to perform any of its functions"
-- is a judgment about the local government's functions and its district, not a
-- question this encoding decides, so it arrives as a fact supplied. The same for
-- the other ten paragraphs of (3): each is encodable, none is encoded here,
-- because the Bill touches only (f).
DECLARE ProposedLocalLaw HAS
    `name`                           IS A STRING
    `made by`                        IS A LocalGovernment

    -- The facts about the law that s 79(3)(f) reads, in the words it uses.
    -- "specifying places where cats are prohibited absolutely" asks three
    -- questions and the current paragraph requires all three.
    `prohibits cats from places`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `the places are specified`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the prohibition is absolute`                    IS A BOOLEAN

    -- The facts the Bill's new s 79(4) names. Each is a thing the new subsection
    -- says a local law "may" do; recording them separately is what lets the two
    -- arms disagree about the same law.
    -- (4)(a)(i)
    `prohibits cats from any or all public places`   IS A BOOLEAN
    -- (4)(a)(ii)
    `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- (4)(b)(i)
    `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- (4)(b)(ii)
    `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- (4)(b)(iii)
    `excepts cats under effective control or contained` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- (4)(c)
    `defines effective control or contained for the local law` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- Example 3 to s 79(4) carves out the journey to the vet. It is an EXAMPLE,
    -- so a local law may omit it; whether it does is the fact that drives
    -- `the local law obstructs compliance with a Part 2 duty` below.
    `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises` IS A BOOLEAN

    -- Facts supplied, per the note above: the parts of s 79 this module does not
    -- decide. Kept explicit rather than assumed, so a case that relies on one
    -- has to say so.
    `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it` IS A BOOLEAN

    -- s 79(2): "A local law made under this Act does not apply outside the local
    -- government's district unless it is made to apply outside the district under
    -- section 80." s 80 is not encoded; both facts are supplied.
    `purports to apply outside the district`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `made to apply outside the district under section 80` IS A BOOLEAN

§§§ `79(3)(f) as in force`

-- "(f) specifying places where cats are prohibited absolutely"
--
-- Three cumulative requirements. The reading taken is that all three bind: the
-- law must prohibit, the places must be specified, and the prohibition must be
-- absolute. A curfew fails the third; a district-wide "anywhere but home" rule
-- fails the second.
--
-- This is the reading the Joint Standing Committee on Delegated Legislation
-- took, as the Bill's own Explanatory Memorandum records: "Prior to this Bill,
-- several local governments had attempted to make cat containment local laws.
-- The Joint Standing Committee on Delegated Legislation (JSCDL) has held the
-- view that the existing powers of the Act did not permit these local laws to be
-- made." The contrary reading -- that the Interpretation Act 1984 s 43(7)-(8)
-- already supplied the conditional and time-limited capacity, so that the Bill
-- only clarifies -- is live, is the reading the EM's own word "clarify" implies,
-- and is recorded as FORK-3. It is not the reading taken here.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 79(3)(f) as in force authorises the local law` IF
        "specifying places"
            ... `the local law`'s `the places are specified`
    AND "where cats are prohibited"
            ... `the local law`'s `prohibits cats from places`
    AND "absolutely"
            ... `the local law`'s `the prohibition is absolute`

§§§ `79(3)(f) and (4) as Bill 53 clause 4 would amend them`

-- Bill 53 cl 4(1): "Delete section 79(3)(f) and insert: (f) the prohibition of
-- cats from places, whether absolutely or otherwise;"
--
-- Two of the three requirements go. What remains is that the law prohibit cats
-- from places. "whether absolutely or otherwise" removes the absoluteness
-- requirement in terms; the new (4)(a) removes the specification requirement by
-- naming "any or all public places" and "any place other than the premises at
-- which the cat is ordinarily kept" as things a law under (f) may do -- neither
-- of which specifies places.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 79(3)(f) as amended by Bill 53 authorises the local law` IF
        "the prohibition of cats from places"
            ... `the local law`'s `prohibits cats from places`

-- Bill 53 cl 4(2), new s 79(4): "Without limiting subsection (3)(f) or the
-- Interpretation Act 1984 section 43(7) or (8), a local law made for the
-- purposes of subsection (3)(f) may -- ...".
--
-- (4) confers nothing: it is an inclusive statement about what (3)(f) already
-- reaches, and it opens with "[w]ithout limiting". So this predicate does not
-- gate validity; it records whether a law is one of the kinds (4) names, which
-- is what makes the amendment's intent demonstrable rather than asserted.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the local law is of a kind section 79(4) names` IF
        "(a)(i) any or all public places"
            ... `the local law`'s `prohibits cats from any or all public places`
    ..  "(a)(ii) any place other than the premises at which the cat is ordinarily kept"
            ... `the local law`'s `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept`
    ..  "(b)(i) applies or does not apply during certain times of day or year or during certain events"
            ... `the local law`'s `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event`
    ..  "(b)(ii) does not apply to a place within the otherwise prohibited area"
            ... `the local law`'s `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area`
    ..  "(b)(iii) does not apply if certain conditions are met, including ... effective control ... or is contained"
            ... `the local law`'s `excepts cats under effective control or contained`
    OR  "(c) provide what constitutes ... effective control ... or being contained"
            ... `the local law`'s `defines effective control or contained for the local law`

§§§ `Validity, under each version`

-- s 79(3) is "[w]ithout limiting subsection (1)", so the heads of power are
-- alternatives: (f), another paragraph of (3), or (1) standing alone.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a head of power in section 79 as in force supports the local law` IF
        `section 79(3)(f) as in force authorises the local law` `the local law`
    ..  "another paragraph of subsection (3)"
            ... `the local law`'s `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it`
    OR  "subsection (1), which subsection (3) does not limit"
            ... `the local law`'s `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)`

GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a head of power in section 79 as amended by Bill 53 supports the local law` IF
        `section 79(3)(f) as amended by Bill 53 authorises the local law` `the local law`
    ..  "another paragraph of subsection (3)"
            ... `the local law`'s `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it`
    OR  "subsection (1), which subsection (3) does not limit"
            ... `the local law`'s `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)`

-- s 79(2) with s 80: a local law does not apply outside the district unless made
-- to do so under s 80. A law that purports to reach outside without s 80 is not
-- void -- it simply does not apply there -- so this is kept as its own predicate
-- rather than folded into validity.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the local law applies outside the district` IF
        `the local law`'s `purports to apply outside the district`
    AND `the local law`'s `made to apply outside the district under section 80`

§§§ `Interpretation Act 1984 (WA) s 43(1) -- inconsistency`

-- Interpretation Act 1984 (WA) s 43(1): "Subsidiary legislation shall not be
-- inconsistent with the provisions of the written law under which it is made, or
-- of any Act, and subsidiary legislation shall be void to the extent of any such
-- inconsistency."
--
-- This is the provision that makes the Part 6 power bite against the Part 2
-- duties this subject encodes, and it is the only place the two meet.
--
-- The interaction: s 18(1) requires the owner to ensure the cat is sterilised BY
-- A VETERINARIAN, and s 14(1) that it is microchipped. Both are discharged by
-- taking the cat to veterinary premises. A local law made under the Bill's new
-- s 79(4)(a)(ii) -- "any place other than the premises at which the cat is
-- ordinarily kept" -- forbids exactly that journey unless it carves it out.
--
-- Example 3 to s 79(4) carves it out. But an example is not a requirement, and
-- nothing in the Bill obliges a local government to copy it. A local law that
-- omits the carve-out makes compliance with a duty in the Act impossible, which
-- is inconsistency, and s 43(1) answers it: the local law is void to that
-- extent. The Act does not need a provision of its own, and does not have one.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the local law obstructs compliance with a Part 2 duty` IF
        "prohibiting a cat from being in any place other than the premises at which the cat is ordinarily kept"
            ... `the local law`'s `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept`
    AND NOT "unless the cat is being transported to, or is at, veterinary premises (Example 3)"
            ... `the local law`'s `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises`

-- Named apart from the obstruction itself, because the obstruction is a fact
-- about the local law and the voidness is the Interpretation Act's consequence.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the local law is void to the extent it obstructs a Part 2 duty` IF
        `the local law obstructs compliance with a Part 2 duty` `the local law`

§§§ `What the Bill changes`

-- The predicate the whole module exists to state. True exactly where Bill 53
-- turns a local law that is beyond power into one that is within it.
GIVEN `the local law` IS A ProposedLocalLaw
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Bill 53 changes the answer for the local law` IF
        `a head of power in section 79 as amended by Bill 53 supports the local law` `the local law`
    AND NOT `a head of power in section 79 as in force supports the local law` `the local law`
